# Karpathy guidelines (coding behavior)

Derived from [andrej-karpathy-skills](https://github.com/multica-ai/andrej-karpathy-skills). Knowledge compounding — see `wiki/` and `wiki-schema.mdc`.

## 1. Think before coding

- State assumptions; if unsure, ask rather than guess.
- When the request is ambiguous, present interpretations — do not pick silently.
- Push back if a simpler approach fits.
- Stop when confused: name what is unclear.

## 2. Simplicity first

- Minimum code that solves the asked problem. Nothing speculative.
- No abstractions for one-use code; no unrequested configurability.
- No error handling for impossible scenarios.
- If it could be 1/3 the size, rewrite.

## 3. Surgical changes

- Touch only what the request requires. Match existing style.
- Do not “improve” adjacent code, comments, or formatting.
- Do not delete pre-existing dead code unless asked; clean up only orphans **your** change created.
- Every changed line should trace to the user request.

## 4. Goal-driven execution

- Prefer verifiable success criteria (tests, repro, checklist) over vague “make it work”.
- For multi-step work, brief plan with a check after each step.
- For trivial one-liners, skip ceremony.

## Tradeoff

Bias caution on non-trivial work; do not slow obvious tiny fixes.
